  justification: 'This lesson helps us to know soil texture, which controls water, air and fertility.',
  activities: [
    { sec: 'I. PARTICLE SIZES', img: 'particle_sizes_big.png', caption: 'The size of soil particles.', q: 'Put sand, silt and clay in order from the largest to the smallest.', a: 'Sand, silt, clay.', noProj: 'show sand, flour and clay.' },
    { sec: 'II. TEXTURAL CLASSES', img: 'texture_triangle_big.png', caption: 'The soil texture triangle.', q: 'A soil has 40 % sand, 40 % silt and 20 % clay. What is its class?', a: 'Loam.', noProj: 'draw the triangle.' },
    { sec: 'III. DETERMINATION', img: 'ribbon_test_big.png', caption: 'The ribbon test for soil texture.', q: 'In the field, how can you tell if a wet soil is sandy or clayey?', a: 'Sand feels gritty and falls apart; clay feels sticky and makes a long ribbon.', noProj: 'rub wet soil between the fingers.' },
    { sec: 'IV. FACTORS', img: 'f4_redsoil.jpg', caption: 'A red tropical soil.', q: 'Why are soils on granite often sandy, and soils on basalt often clayey?', a: 'Granite weathers into quartz sand; basalt weathers into clay.', noProj: 'compare local rocks.' },
    { sec: 'V. SIGNIFICANCE', img: 'f2t_rice_field.jpg', caption: 'A rice field.', q: 'Why is rice grown on clay soils?', a: 'Clay holds water and keeps the field flooded.', noProj: 'mention rice at Ndop and Yagoua.' },
  ],
  summary: [
    { title: 'I. Definitions', items: [['Soil texture:', 'It is the proportion of sand, silt and clay in a soil.'], ['Loam:', 'It is a soil with a good mixture of sand, silt and clay.'], ['Texture triangle:', 'It is a triangle graph used to name the texture class of a soil.']] },
    { title: 'II. Classes and Determination', items: [['A. Particle sizes:', 'Sand is 0.05–2 mm, silt 0.002–0.05 mm and clay below 0.002 mm. Gravel is bigger than 2 mm.'], ['B. Field methods:', 'We feel, mould and roll wet soil in the hand. Sand is gritty, silt is smooth and clay is sticky.'], ['C. Laboratory methods:', 'Sieving and sedimentation separate the particles. The percentages are plotted on the texture triangle.']] },
    { title: 'III. Factors and Significance', items: [['A. Factors:', 'Parent rock, weathering and age control texture. Water and wind also move fine particles.'], ['B. Sandy soils:', 'They are light, well aerated and easy to dig. But they drain fast and lose nutrients.'], ['C. Clay and loam soils:', 'Clay holds water and nutrients but can be sticky and waterlogged. Loam is the best balance for most crops.']], draw: { img: 'texture_triangle_big.png', caption: 'Copy the texture triangle.' } },
  ],
  evaluation: [['What is soil texture?', 'The proportion of sand, silt and clay in a soil.'], ['Give one advantage and one disadvantage of a sandy soil.', 'Advantage: easy to dig and well aerated. Disadvantage: dries fast and loses nutrients.']],
  remediation: ['The smallest soil particles are ______.', 'A soil with a good mix of particles is ______.', 'Texture classes are read on the texture ______.'], remediationAnswers: '1. clay 2. loam 3. triangle',
